.TH "RUSTICPROFILE" "1" "August 2026" "rusticprofile 0.2.29" "Backup Scheduler"

.SH "NAME"
.PP
rusticprofile \- a local, per\-machine scheduler and orchestrator for rustic backups

.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBrusticprofile\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP]
.PP
\fBrusticprofile config\fP \fB\-\-check\fP [\fB\-\-as\-host\fP \fIHOST\fP] [\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP]
.PP
\fBrusticprofile config\fP \fB\-\-show\fP \fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP [\fB\-\-as\-host\fP \fIHOST\fP] [\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP]
.PP
\fBrusticprofile run\fP \fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fP] [\fB\-\-json\fP] [\fB\-\-background\fP] [\fB\-\-rustic\-binary\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-as\-host\fP \fIHOST\fP] [\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP]
.PP
\fBrusticprofile schedule\fP [\fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP] [\fB\-\-write\-only\fP] [\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-unit\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP]
.PP
\fBrusticprofile unschedule\fP \fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP [\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-unit\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP]
.PP
\fBrusticprofile status\fP [\fB\-\-json\fP] [\fB\-\-as\-host\fP \fIHOST\fP] [\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-unit\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP]
.PP
\fBrusticprofile snapshots\fP \fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP [\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-as\-host\fP \fIHOST\fP] [\fB\-\-rustic\-binary\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-\fP \fIRUSTIC ARGS\fP]
.PP
\fBrusticprofile retention\fP \fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP [\fB\-\-near\fP \fIDATE\fP] [\fB\-\-all\fP] [\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-as\-host\fP \fIHOST\fP] [\fB\-\-rustic\-binary\fP \fIPATH\fP]
.PP
\fBrusticprofile plan\fP \fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP [\fB\-\-format\fP \fIFORMAT\fP] [\fB\-\-show\-env\fP [\fB\-\-show\-secrets\fP]] [\fB\-\-as\-host\fP \fIHOST\fP] [\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP]
.PP
\fBrusticprofile doctor\fP [\fB\-\-repository\fP] [\fB\-\-json\fP] [\fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP] [\fB\-\-as\-host\fP \fIHOST\fP] [\fB\-\-rustic\-binary\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP]

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBrusticprofile\fP decides \fIwhen\fP backups run, \fIwhich\fP jobs exist, and \fIon which hosts\fP\. It installs and manages the systemd units (or launchd agents) that trigger them, sequences the operations within a job, classifies what \fBrustic\fP(1) reports back, and coordinates locks against a shared repository\.
.PP
It does \fBnot\fP configure backups\. The repository, source paths, excludes, retention policy, hooks, environment and metrics all live in rustic's own `\f[CR]rustic\.toml\fP`, which covers them natively\. rusticprofile constructs no backup flags: a job resolves to `\f[CR]rustic \-P <profile> <operation>\fP`, plus `\f[CR]\-\-json\fP` and one `\f[CR]\-\-name\fP` for each snapshot set enabled on the current host\.
.PP
This separation is deliberate\. rustic already provides profiles, hooks, forget policies and Prometheus metrics; a wrapper that re\-specified them would mostly reinvent rustic\. The gap this fills is local OS\-level scheduling with per\-host variation and no central server\.
.PP
The \fBconfig\fP subcommand inspects and validates `\f[CR]jobs\.yaml\fP`, and the \fBplan\fP subcommand prints the exact command line a job would run without running it\. Neither ever invokes \fBrustic\fP(1), contacts a repository, or needs a network, so both are safe to run at any time\.
.PP
The \fBrun\fP subcommand executes a job, and \fBschedule\fP installs the systemd units (Linux) or launchd agent (macOS) that triggers it\. Invoking \fBrusticprofile\fP with no arguments exits non\-zero and says so rather than doing anything by default\.

.SH "OPTIONS"
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fP
.RS
Show help information\.
.RE
\fB\-V, \-\-version\fP
.RS
Print version information\.
.RE
\fB\-\-completions\fP \fISHELL\fP
.RS
Write a shell completion script to standard output\. \fISHELL\fP is one of `\f[CR]bash\fP`, `\f[CR]zsh\fP`, `\f[CR]fish\fP`, `\f[CR]elvish\fP`, `\f[CR]nushell\fP` or `\f[CR]power\-shell\fP`\.
.RE

.SH "CONFIG"
\fB\-\-check\fP
.RS
.PP
Validate the configuration\. \fBEvery problem is reported at once\fP, not one per run, and nothing is spawned before validation completes\. Exits 0 if the configuration is usable, 2 otherwise\. Also prints which jobs this host runs and which are excluded by \fBenabled\-on\-hosts\fP \-\- a gate that cannot be seen is indistinguishable from a job that was never written\.
.RE
\fB\-\-show\fP \fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP
.RS
.PP
Print the \fBeffective\fP configuration for one job, which is two files rather than one\.
.PP
First the job itself: its operations, the snapshot sets that survive host gating, its schedule and its log path\. If \fIJOB\fP exists but is gated off on this host, that is reported as such rather than as an unknown job\.
.PP
Then the rustic profile it delegates to, read\-only: the repository, which password mechanism is in use, `\f[CR]group\-by\fP`, the `\f[CR]keep\-*\fP` retention rules, which scoping filters are declared, and each snapshot set with its label and how many sources it lists\. \fBThe delegation boundary puts nearly everything that can silently lose data in that second file\fP, so printing only the first half told you about the half that cannot\. A set with no `\f[CR]label\fP` is called out, because an unlabelled set shares one retention group with every other unlabelled snapshot in the repository \-\- including another tool's\.
.PP
It does \fBnot\fP echo the profile; \fBcat\fP(1) does that better, and a partial reprint of rustic's format would go stale the moment rustic adds a key\. Only what rusticprofile already understands well enough to validate is reported\. Nothing in the profile is written, and the secret\-bearing keys are read as \fIpaths\fP, never contents\.
.PP
A profile that cannot be read is named as such rather than left blank \-\- \fBconfig \-\-check\fP is the command that refuses\.
.RE
\fB\-\-example\fP \fIWHAT\fP
.RS
.PP
Write an annotated starting\-point configuration to stdout, where \fIWHAT\fP is \fBjobs\fP or \fBrustic\fP\. Requires no existing configuration and reads nothing \-\- it is what you ask for when you have neither file yet\.
.PP
\fBjobs\fP is the small one: rusticprofile owns only which jobs exist, what they run, on which hosts, and when\. \fBrustic\fP is the one that matters, because the delegation boundary puts nearly everything that can silently lose data in rustic's own config \-\- the exclusion globs that are \fIinclude\fP filters unless prefixed with `\f[CR]!\fP`, the scoping filter that rustic accepts and ignores if placed under \fB[forget]\fP instead of \fB[snapshot\-filter]\fP, and the retention grouping that lets a 0\-byte snapshot evict a 395 MiB one\. Each annotation records a measured failure, not a preference\.
.PP
Emitted to stdout rather than written anywhere, exactly as \fB\-\-completions\fP is: placing the file is a deliberate act, and this command cannot overwrite a working configuration\. Redirect it yourself\.
.PP
Placeholders (`\f[CR]host\-a\fP`, `\f[CR]/home/user\fP`) are \fBstatic\fP\. Your hostname and home directory are deliberately not filled in \-\- a configuration that appears to work is one nobody reads, and every value in these files is worth reading once\.
.RE
\fB\-\-as\-host\fP \fIHOST\fP
.RS
.PP
Evaluate as though running on \fIHOST\fP instead of the real hostname\. This is the only way to check another machine's view of a per\-host gate without logging into it\.
.RE
.PP
A configuration may set \fBdefaults\.default\-job\fP, which is the job used by \fBrun\fP, \fBplan\fP, \fBsnapshots\fP, \fBretention\fP and \fBconfig \-\-show\fP when `\f[CR]\-n\fP` is omitted\. It is validated at load time against the \fIdeclared\fP jobs, so a typo is an error on every machine rather than only where that job happens to run\.
.PP
Two commands deliberately ignore it\. \fBunschedule\fP always requires an explicit name \-\- removing a schedule because a configuration file named a default, rather than because someone typed it, is the one action that should never happen by default\. \fBschedule\fP already treats a missing `\f[CR]\-n\fP` as "every job that declares a schedule", which is useful in its own right and would be lost\.
\fB\-\-config\fP \fIPATH\fP
.RS
Read \fIPATH\fP instead of the default `\f[CR]jobs\.yaml\fP`\.
.RE
.PP
Validation also rejects a `\f[CR]sources\fP` entry containing `\f[CR]~\fP` or `\f[CR]$\fP` in a profile a job backs up with\. rustic expands neither, and the failure is silent rather than loud: the literal string is a \fIrelative\fP path, so it misses the hard error an absent absolute path produces\. rustic warns, backs up nothing, saves a 0\-byte snapshot and exits 0 \-\- and that empty snapshot then wins its retention slot against the real one\. A `\f[CR]rustic\.toml\fP` therefore cannot be shared unmodified between hosts and must be generated per host; `\f[CR]jobs\.yaml\fP` has no such limitation and is identical everywhere\.
.PP
Validation rejects, among other things, any key it does not recognise; a snapshot set that is not defined in the rustic profile it names; a job whose snapshot sets are all gated away on this host; an \fBenabled\-on\-hosts\fP list that is empty; a relative log path; and an unknown or malformed `\f[CR]${\.\.\.}\fP` reference\. Each of these would otherwise cause the tool to quietly do less than the configuration says\.

.SH "RUN"
\fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP
.RS
.PP
The job to run\. Required \-\- there is no "run everything" form\.
.RE
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fP
.RS
.PP
Ask rustic to report what it would do without writing anything\. Reports what \fIwould\fP be saved, never what was saved\.
.RE
\fB\-\-background\fP
.RS
.PP
Mark the run as unattended, i\.e\. started by the scheduler rather than typed\. \fBschedule\fP puts this in every generated Task Scheduler definition and in every generated systemd service, and it does two things \-\- one on every platform and one only on Windows\.
.PP
Everywhere, it enables the \fBretry\fP: a failed operation is attempted twice more at two\-minute intervals before the job stops\. This exists because a missed calendar time is replayed within \fIseconds\fP of the machine waking, which is before the network is back, so the run that replaces a missed hour is the one most likely to fail\. Measured on both platforms; on Linux two catch\-ups each fired in the same second as a resume and failed on DNS, while the network became usable 11 and 12 seconds later\. Only a plain failure is retried \-\- an interrupted run is a decision, a partial one already continues to \fBforget\fP, and \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP never waits\. The retry is stated in the operation's summary, so a retried run cannot read as a first\-attempt success\. A run you type yourself is unaffected and fails immediately, which is the whole reason this is a flag rather than something inferred: on Linux `\f[CR]INVOCATION_ID\fP` and `\f[CR]JOURNAL_STREAM\fP` are both set in an ordinary terminal, so the environment cannot distinguish the two cases\.
.PP
On \fBWindows only\fP, it additionally detaches from the console so the run shows no window\. Task Scheduler can run a task as the logged\-on user only through an interactive logon, which starts it inside that desktop session, where Windows gives a console program a console; the logon types that run outside it need rights an ordinary account does not hold\. Typing it by hand there discards the run's output \-\- rustic's progress goes nowhere \-\- while the record still reaches the \fBlog:\fP file\. On Unix it touches no stream, so a scheduled run's diagnostics still reach the journal\.
.PP
macOS is not covered by the retry: the same race is plausible under launchd and has not been measured\.
.RE
\fB\-\-rustic\-binary\fP \fIPATH\fP
.RS
.PP
Use this executable instead of the one the configuration names\. A bare name is resolved on `\f[CR]PATH\fP`\. Applied after the configuration is validated, so it cannot mask a mistake in the file it overrides\. Point it at a script that records its arguments and exits, and a job can be exercised end to end without rustic running at all\.
.RE
.PP
Operations run in the order the job lists them\. A failed operation stops the job; a \fBpartial\fP one does not\. A backup that saved some of its snapshot sets and failed on others still proceeds to \fBforget\fP, because skipping retention after a partly\-successful backup is how snapshots accumulate without limit\. A backup that saved \fInothing\fP does stop the job, since running retention after it would delete old snapshots with no new ones in place\. Operations that did not run are listed explicitly in the summary\.
.PP
A local lock prevents the same job running twice on this machine, and is not taken by waiting: a second run is refused immediately rather than queued\. It says nothing about other machines sharing the repository \-\- repository\-level lock coordination is a later milestone\.
.PP
\fBThat milestone is not a precondition for `\f[CR]prune\fP`, and an earlier version of this page said it was\.\fP rustic is lock\-free \fIby design\fP: its `\f[CR]prune\fP` marks packs and deletes them only after `\f[CR]\-\-keep\-delete\fP`, 23 hours by default, so a concurrent rustic backup has a day of grace\. The measured hazard is narrower and is about mixing tools \-\- \fBnever run `\f[CR]restic prune\fP` against a repository any rustic client writes to\fP, because restic deletes packs immediately, which is safe only by virtue of an exclusive repository lock that rustic neither takes nor honours\. `\f[CR]rustic prune\fP` is safe and is the only prune that should run against a shared repository\.
.PP
Exit status is \fB0\fP for success or partial, \fB1\fP for failure, \fB130\fP for interruption\.

.SH "SCHEDULE"
.PP
\fBschedule\fP installs a job's OS schedule and arms it; \fBunschedule\fP disarms and removes it\. Each is a single step and each fully undoes the other\. \fBstatus\fP reports what is installed on this host and what is deliberately not\.
.PP
On \fBLinux\fP that means systemd units driven by `\f[CR]systemctl\fP`; on \fBmacOS\fP, a launchd agent driven by `\f[CR]launchctl\fP`\. On a platform with neither, \fBschedule\fP refuses and writes nothing \-\- files on disk plus a success message are indistinguishable from a working install, so refusing is the only honest answer\. \fBstatus\fP reports the backend it found, and `\f[CR]status \-\-json\fP` names it in a `\f[CR]backend\fP` field\.
.PP
On \fBWindows\fP it means one Task Scheduler task under `\f[CR]\rusticprofile\\fP`\fIJOB\fP, driven by `\f[CR]schtasks\fP`\. Where there is no backend at all, \fBstatus\fP still prints each job's `\f[CR]last run\fP` and `\f[CR]last success\fP`, because that record is written by \fBrun\fP and does not come from a service manager; it is the field to watch in any case, since a run that never happens reports nothing\.
.PP
\fBstatus\fP prints `\f[CR]last run\fP` and `\f[CR]last success\fP` in the same shape the service manager reports `\f[CR]next run\fP` \-\- `\f[CR]Tue 2026\-08\-11 20:28:57 PDT\fP` \-\- and in this machine's own time zone, so the three lines describe one clock and can be read against each other\. \fBThe record itself is unchanged\fP: the status file and `\f[CR]status \-\-json\fP` both keep RFC 3339, which is what a monitor parses\. Where no time\-zone database is available the zone cannot be named, so the offset the record carries is shown instead (`\f[CR]Tue 2026\-08\-11 20:28:57 \-07:00\fP`); a stamp this version cannot parse is printed exactly as it was stored rather than dropped\.
.PP
On \fBWindows\fP `\f[CR]next run\fP` is rendered into that same shape too, and reads `\f[CR]Wed 2026\-08\-12 11:02:28 PDT (+\-5 min)\fP`\. Two details behind it are worth knowing, because both are properties of Task Scheduler rather than of this tool\. The instant is obtained from `\f[CR]Get\-ScheduledTaskInfo\fP` via Windows PowerShell, because the value `\f[CR]schtasks\fP` prints is formatted in the account's locale and `\f[CR]8/12/2026\fP` means two different dates depending on it \-\- so that string is never parsed, and where the lookup is unavailable the line falls back to printing it verbatim rather than risking a wrong date\. And `\f[CR](+\-5 min)\fP` is the schedule's own randomised spread, which Task Scheduler \fBre\-rolls on every query\fP: two `\f[CR]status\fP` invocations seconds apart legitimately report times a few minutes apart, so the window is stated rather than implying a precision that is not there\. `\f[CR]status \-\-json\fP` is unaffected \-\- `\f[CR]next_run\fP` stays exactly what the service manager reported\. The window is not printed on Linux or macOS, where no such re\-roll has been measured\.
\fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP
.RS
The job to act on\. Optional for \fBschedule\fP, where omitting it installs every job declaring a `\f[CR]schedule:\fP` block\. Required for \fBunschedule\fP \-\- removal is always named explicitly\.
.RE
\fB\-\-write\-only\fP
.RS
Write the schedule without arming it\. `\f[CR]schedule\fP` arms by default \-\- that is what the verb means, and \fBunschedule\fP is a single step that fully undoes it\. Use this to inspect what was generated before anything can fire\.
.RE
\fB\-\-unit\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP
.RS
Write or read units and agents in \fIDIR\fP instead of the platform's own directory\. Intended for inspecting what is generated without installing it; neither `\f[CR]systemctl\fP` nor `\f[CR]launchctl\fP` is invoked when this is given\.
.RE
.PP
Both commands are idempotent on both platforms: identical content is not rewritten, and removing something that is not there is not an error\. Re\-running \fBschedule\fP reports `\f[CR]unchanged\fP` rather than implying work happened\.
.PP
Nothing generated contains a \fBlog path or a date\fP\. A unit or agent written today must not log to today's file forever, so `\f[CR]${date:\.\.\.}\fP` is resolved per run rather than at install time\.

.SS "systemd (Linux)"
.PP
A scheduled job is \fBtwo\fP units, `\f[CR]rusticprofile\-\fP`\fIJOB\fP`\f[CR]\.service\fP` and `\f[CR]\.timer\fP`\. systemd offers no way for a timer to run a command directly, so this is not a choice rusticprofile makes\. Only the timer carries an `\f[CR][Install]\fP` section; the service is reported as `\f[CR]static\fP`, meaning it can be started by its timer and not enabled independently \-\- a service that could be enabled on its own would run the backup at every login, with no schedule to explain it\.
.PP
The timer sets \fBPersistent=true\fP, so a run missed while the machine was asleep is caught up rather than silently skipped, and \fBRandomizedDelaySec\fP, so several machines sharing one repository do not all wake on the same instant\. Priority is expressed as `\f[CR]Nice=\fP` and `\f[CR]IOSchedulingClass=\fP` in the unit rather than applied in\-process\.

.SS "Task Scheduler (Windows)"
.PP
A scheduled job is \fBone\fP task, registered at `\f[CR]\rusticprofile\\fP`\fIJOB\fP rather than written into a directory a service manager reads: `\f[CR]schtasks /Create /XML\fP` copies the definition into the service's own store\. rusticprofile keeps the definition it generated under `\f[CR]$XDG_STATE_HOME/rusticprofile/tasks\fP` as a record, and the service remains the authority on what is actually scheduled\. `\f[CR]permission: system\fP` registers the task under the LocalSystem account instead of your own\.
.PP
Three things differ from the Unix backends, each measured rather than assumed:
.PP
\fBHourly is twenty\-four triggers, not one repeating trigger\.\fP A repeating trigger whose start boundary lies in the past is treated by Task Scheduler as \fIcurrently due\fP and runs the task the moment it is registered \-\- which would make \fBschedule\fP take a backup, and run retention, as a side effect of scheduling\. Twenty\-four plain daily triggers, one per hour, avoid that while still firing within the hour\.
.PP
\fBBoth priorities are written out\.\fP `\f[CR]priority: standard\fP` emits `\f[CR]<Priority>5</Priority>\fP` rather than nothing, because Task Scheduler's own default is 7 \-\- already below normal \-\- so silence would quietly mean "de\-prioritised" here while meaning "leave the default alone" on the other two platforms\.
.PP
\fBTwo Task Scheduler defaults are overridden\fP, because both would stop backups without reporting anything: `\f[CR]DisallowStartIfOnBatteries\fP` and `\f[CR]StopIfGoingOnBatteries\fP` are `\f[CR]true\fP` by default, so a laptop on battery would take no backups and unplugging mid\-run would kill one\. `\f[CR]ExecutionTimeLimit\fP` is also cleared, since the default of three days would terminate a long first backup rather than let it finish\.
.PP
A user task, like a launchd agent, runs \fBonly while you are logged on\fP \-\- there is no `\f[CR]linger\fP` equivalent\. `\f[CR]permission: system\fP` runs regardless, as SYSTEM; running as yourself without a login session would need a stored password or S4U, which relocates the credential problem rather than removing it\.

.SS "launchd (macOS)"
.PP
A scheduled job is \fBone\fP agent, `\f[CR]~/Library/LaunchAgents/local\.rusticprofile\.\fP`\fIJOB\fP`\f[CR]\.plist\fP`, because launchd puts the schedule and the program in the same job\. `\f[CR]permission: system\fP` installs a LaunchDaemon under `\f[CR]/Library/LaunchDaemons\fP` instead\. Priority becomes `\f[CR]ProcessType=Background\fP`, `\f[CR]Nice\fP` and the `\f[CR]LowPriorityIO\fP` keys \-\- again in the agent, not applied in\-process\.
.PP
Four differences from systemd are worth knowing, because each is a property of launchd rather than of this tool:
.PP
\fBThe fleet spread is a real minute\.\fP launchd has no `\f[CR]RandomizedDelaySec\fP`, so the offset is part of `\f[CR]StartCalendarInterval\fP` and \fBschedule\fP prints it (`\f[CR]runs hourly at 7 past\fP`)\. It is chosen once, on first install, and reused afterwards \-\- so re\-running \fBschedule\fP neither moves your slot nor reports a change\. It is bounded by the same window systemd is given, so `\f[CR]at: hourly\fP` means the same thing on both platforms\.
.PP
\fBSleep is handled; an unloaded agent is not\.\fP `\f[CR]launchd\.plist\fP`(5) states that a job whose calendar time passes while the machine is asleep runs when it next wakes, with multiple missed intervals coalesced into one \-\- which is what systemd's `\f[CR]Persistent=true\fP` provides\. A calendar time that passes while the agent is \fInot loaded\fP is not caught up, so the first run after \fBschedule\fP is at the next occurrence rather than immediately\.
.PP
\fBA user agent runs only while you are logged in\.\fP launchd has no equivalent of systemd's `\f[CR]linger\fP`, so a Mac sitting at the login window takes no backups at all: nothing fails, nothing is logged, and the only evidence is an absence\. \fBschedule\fP and \fBstatus\fP both say so\. Watch `\f[CR]last success\fP` rather than the schedule, or use `\f[CR]permission: system\fP`, which runs regardless of login \-\- as root, which needs its own answer for repository credentials\.
.PP
\fBThere is no next\-fire time to report\.\fP `\f[CR]launchctl print\fP` gives the calendar descriptor and never a next firing, so `\f[CR]next run\fP` reads \fInot reported by launchd\fP and the JSON `\f[CR]next_run\fP` is `\f[CR]null\fP`\. Computing one and presenting it as launchd's would be inventing a fact about a schedule\.
\fB\-\-json\fP
.RS
.PP
Emit the report as JSON instead of the human summary, on \fBrun\fP and \fBstatus\fP\. For anything automated: matching the human summary would mean matching English, which is exactly what rusticprofile refuses to do to rustic's own output and for the same reason \-\- a summary line is a message to a person and changes when the wording improves\.
.PP
The object opens with a `\f[CR]schema\fP` number\. Fields may be \fBadded\fP without changing it; anything removed or given a new meaning bumps it, so a consumer that ignores unknown fields keeps working\.
.PP
\fB`\f[CR]last_success\fP` is the field worth alerting on\.\fP A schedule can be armed, green and firing while every run fails, and a disabled timer fails nothing at all \-\- neither shows up in `\f[CR]enabled\fP` or `\f[CR]next_run\fP`\. `\f[CR]null\fP` there means the job has never succeeded\. Note `\f[CR]enabled\fP` and `\f[CR]active\fP` are likewise `\f[CR]null\fP` for "could not tell", which is not the same as `\f[CR]false\fP`: only `\f[CR]false\fP` means the schedule is definitely off\.
.PP
rustic's own progress output goes to stderr, so `\f[CR]rusticprofile run \-\-json 2>/dev/null\fP` is parseable as\-is\.
.RE
.PP
\fBstatus\fP distinguishes \fIinstalled but not enabled\fP from \fIstate unknown\fP, since only the former means the schedule is definitely off, and it lists jobs excluded by \fBenabled\-on\-hosts\fP so that a host without a given timer reads as a decision rather than an absence\.

.SH "PLAN"
\fB\-n\fP \fIJOB\fP
.RS
The job to plan\. Required \-\- there is no "plan everything" form\.
.RE
\fB\-\-format\fP \fIFORMAT\fP
.RS
`\f[CR]human\fP` (default) prints a readable summary, one operation per line\. `\f[CR]lines\fP` prints one argv element per line, with a blank line between operations; this is the exact form, and is what the project's golden tests record\.
.RE
\fB\-\-show\-env\fP
.RS
Also print the rustic\-related environment the run would inherit, with secret values masked\. Not valid with `\f[CR]\-\-format lines\fP`, which is an exact machine\-readable form that extra output would corrupt\. rusticprofile does not modify the environment; this shows what rustic will receive\.
.RE
\fB\-\-show\-secrets\fP
.RS
Print secret values in full instead of masking them\. Requires \fB\-\-show\-env\fP, and prints a warning to standard error first\. Anything capturing the terminal captures the secrets too\.
.RE
.PP
The argv is the whole of what rusticprofile constructs:
.RS
.PP
.nf
rustic \-P RESOLVED\-PROFILE\-PATH OPERATION [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-json] [\-\-name SET]\.\.\.
.fi
.RE
.PP
`\f[CR]\-P\fP` is given a resolved absolute path rather than a bare profile name, because rustic's own search for a bare name need not include the directory rusticprofile validated against\. `\f[CR]\-\-name\fP` appears only for \fBbackup\fP, once per snapshot set enabled on the current host, as does `\f[CR]\-\-json\fP`\. Those three flags plus `\f[CR]\-P\fP` are the only ones rusticprofile ever emits; everything else a backup needs comes from rustic's own configuration\. `\f[CR]\-\-json\fP` is requested because rustic exits 1 for both a partial and a failed backup, and the count of snapshot objects it writes to standard output is the only reliable way to tell them apart\. Progress and diagnostics are unaffected \-\- rustic writes those to standard error\.

.SH "SNAPSHOTS"
\fBsnapshots \-n\fP \fIJOB\fP
.RS
.PP
List the repository's snapshots by handing the query to \fBrustic\fP(1)\.
.PP
\fBThis is a read\-only passthrough, and the only thing rusticprofile contributes is the resolved profile path\fP \-\- the value you would otherwise have to remember and type\. Arguments after `\f[CR]\-\-\fP` are given to rustic unchanged; rusticprofile constructs none of them, and rustic's exit code is passed straight through rather than replaced with a verdict of our own\.
.RS
.PP
.nf
rusticprofile snapshots \-n dot\-files
rusticprofile snapshots \-n dot\-files \-\- \-\-filter\-label core
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read\-only is what makes this defensible\. There is deliberately no passthrough for \fBforget\fP or \fBprune\fP, which are destructive and whose scoping belongs in the rustic profile where a flag typed at a prompt cannot contradict it, nor for \fBrestore\fP \-\- putting a restore path behind a scheduler adds a layer between you and your data at the moment you least want one\. `\f[CR]snapshots\fP` is also \fBnot\fP an operation a job may schedule; that set remains \fBbackup\fP, \fBforget\fP and \fBprune\fP\.
.PP
A snapshot listing cannot say which snapshot is the current \fIyearly\fP one, because retention is not something \fBrustic snapshots\fP knows about\. See \fBRETENTION\fP\.
.RE

.SH "RETENTION"
\fBretention \-n\fP \fIJOB\fP [\fB\-\-near\fP \fIDATE\fP] [\fB\-\-all\fP]\fP
.RS
.PP
Show how far back each retention period still reaches, what a \fBforget\fP would remove, and \-\- with \fB\-\-near\fP \-\- which snapshots sit either side of a date\.
.PP
\fBThis is a restore\-hunting tool as much as a retention one\.\fP Retention makes snapshot density non\-uniform: the last day is available hour by hour, last week day by day, last year month by month\. So the question \fI"I need a file from around then"\fP has a second half \-\- \fIhow close to then can I actually get?\fP \-\- and that is what this reports\.
.PP
\fBrustic computes this and nothing is recomputed here\.\fP `\f[CR]rustic forget \-\-dry\-run\fP` reports a \fIreason\fP per kept snapshot \-\- `\f[CR]hourly\fP`, `\f[CR]daily\fP`, `\f[CR]weekly\fP`, `\f[CR]monthly\fP`, `\f[CR]yearly\fP`, `\f[CR]last\fP`, `\f[CR]tags\fP`, `\f[CR]id\fP` \-\- and this command resolves the job's profile, asks for that in JSON, groups it per retention group and reports how far back each period reaches\. The reason words are rustic's own, passed through unchanged: rustic offers 23 `\f[CR]keep\-*\fP` options including `\f[CR]quarter\-yearly\fP`, `\f[CR]half\-yearly\fP` and twelve `\f[CR]within\-*\fP` forms, and a fixed list here would silently drop whichever one you configure next\.
.RS
.PP
.nf
rusticprofile retention \-n dot\-files
rusticprofile retention \-n dot\-files \-\-near 2026\-05\-15
rusticprofile retention \-n dot\-files \-\-all
.fi
.RE
.PP
Taken from a real run, with the paths and the hostname replaced by this page's placeholders:
.RS
.PP
.nf
retention: dot\-files
  profile        /home/user/\.config/rustic/dot\-files\.toml
  group\-by       host,label
  policy         keep\-daily 7, keep\-hourly 24, keep\-monthly 12, keep\-weekly 4, keep\-yearly 2
  scoped to      \-\-filter\-host host\-a
  dry run        yes \- nothing in the repository is changed by this command

  host host\-a / label (none)   38 snapshots, 38 kept, 0 would be removed
    newest kept    0892a9f2  Mon 2026\-08\-03 10:00:43 PDT
    resolution available, oldest snapshot holding each period
      hourly          24 held   back to 8e843752  Tue 2026\-07\-28 20:00:08 PDT
      daily            7 held   back to 31ca4661  Thu 2026\-07\-23 23:00:05 PDT
      weekly           4 held   back to 885b2ae6  Tue 2026\-07\-14 06:00:21 PDT
      monthly         12 held   back to fbe61648  Tue 2025\-09\-30 18:00:22 PDT
      yearly           2 held   back to fb265bd8  Wed 2025\-12\-31 12:23:17 PST
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read as: anything newer than 28 July can be targeted to the hour, back to 14 July to the week, and before that one snapshot a month \-\- with 2025\-09\-30 the floor\.
.PP
\fBThe newest holder is printed once, not once per period, and that is deliberate\.\fP The newest snapshot in a group is simultaneously its hourly, its daily, its monthly \fIand\fP its yearly holder, so a per\-period "newest" column repeats one fact and answers nothing\. What differs per period, and what is worth reading, is how far back it reaches\. A period whose newest holder is \fInot\fP the group's newest kept snapshot is annotated on its own line, since the elision would then be hiding something\.
.RE
\fB\-\-near\fP \fIDATE\fP
.RS
.PP
Show the snapshots either side of \fIDATE\fP, how far off each is, and which periods hold them\.
.RS
.PP
.nf
$ rusticprofile retention \-\-near 2026\-05\-15

retention: dot\-files      near Fri 2026\-05\-15 00:00:00 PDT
  \.\.\.
  host host\-a / label (none)   38 snapshots, 38 kept, 0 would be removed
    nearest before   f93b52fc  Thu 2026\-04\-30 17:00:05 PDT      14d 6h earlier  (monthly)
    nearest after    27d99f0d  Wed 2026\-05\-27 17:00:16 PDT     12d 17h later    (monthly)

  host host\-a / label core   29 snapshots, 29 kept, 0 would be removed
    nearest before   (none \- this group has no snapshot at or before that date)
    nearest after    00ba75f9  Mon 2026\-08\-03 12:04:45 PDT     80d 12h later    (weekly monthly yearly)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The reasons in brackets are the resolution tier you have landed in, which tells you whether looking for something closer is worth the effort\. `\f[CR](monthly)\fP` either side means nothing nearer exists\.
.PP
\fIDATE\fP may be `\f[CR]2026\-05\-15\fP`, `\f[CR]"2026\-05\-15 14:30"\fP`, `\f[CR]2026\-05\-15T14:30:00\fP`, or a value carrying its own offset\. A bare date or date\-time is read in this machine's time zone \-\- the one every time on screen is printed in\. An unreadable \fIDATE\fP is refused \fBbefore\fP rustic is spawned, so a typo costs nothing\.
.PP
\fBSnapshots the policy would remove are included\fP, labelled as such\. A dry run has removed nothing, so they are still places a file can be recovered from, and excluding them would silently answer a narrower question\.
.PP
A group with no snapshot on one side says so rather than substituting the nearest on the other\. On a migrated label that absence \fIis\fP the finding: it marks the cutover\.
.PP
For an exact query rather than a survey \-\- "the single newest snapshot at or before this date" \-\- rustic answers it directly through the read\-only passthrough, and no part of it is reimplemented here:
.RS
.PP
.nf
rusticprofile snapshots \-n dot\-files \-\- \
    \-\-group\-by host,label \-\-filter\-before 2026\-05\-15 \-\-filter\-last 1
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBPin `\f[CR]\-\-group\-by\fP`\.\fP rustic's default groups by `\f[CR]paths\fP` as well, so the obvious form returns one answer per distinct source list \-\- three rows where you expected one, all of them correct and none of them the answer\.
.PP
\fBWhich snapshot holds a slot is not something you pick\.\fP rustic derives it from the `\f[CR]keep\-*\fP` rules in the profile's \fB[forget]\fP section \-\- the ones printed on the `\f[CR]policy\fP` line\. For each period it keeps the \fBnewest\fP snapshot falling in that period, for as many periods back as the rule allows, so `\f[CR]keep\-monthly = 12\fP` holds the newest snapshot of each of the last twelve months\. Changing which snapshots are held therefore means changing that policy, in `\f[CR]rustic\.toml\fP`: \fBrusticprofile emits no retention flags of its own\fP, which is what makes the profile the single place this is decided\.
.PP
To hold a \fIparticular\fP snapshot regardless of period, rustic has two rules that name snapshots instead of periods, and both are ordinary `\f[CR][forget]\fP` keys:
\fBkeep\-tags\fP
.RS
Keep any snapshot carrying one of these tags \-\- tagged at backup time with `\f[CR]rustic backup \-\-tag\fP`, or afterwards with `\f[CR]rustic tag\fP`\. It then appears here with the reason \fBtags\fP\.
.RE
\fBkeep\-id\fP
.RS
Keep snapshots whose id starts with a given prefix, in the 8\-character form this report prints\. Appears with the reason \fBid\fP\.
.RE
.PP
\fBkeep\-delete\fP is \fInot\fP one of these\. It is prune's grace period, and rustic rejects a `\f[CR][forget]\fP` declaring only that\.
.PP
\fBAnchors are reported per retention group, not per repository\.\fP With the `\f[CR]group\-by = "host,label"\fP` that a job using named snapshot sets requires, each set is its own group with its own slots, so there is no single fleet\-wide "yearly snapshot" to name\.
.PP
\fBThis is always a dry run, and not because a flag says so\.\fP The command line is built from the same code path the scheduled \fBforget\fP uses, with `\f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\fP` fixed in place and no parameter that could omit it \-\- so the preview cannot describe a different operation than the one that runs, and the command cannot be turned into one that deletes\. It carries `\f[CR]\-\-filter\-host\fP` for the same reason: the scheduled \fBforget\fP does, and a preview showing the whole fleet's retention while the job only touches this host would be worse than no preview\. Measured against rustic 0\.11\.3: a dry\-run \fBforget\fP leaves every file in the repository byte\-identical\.
.PP
Snapshots that \fBwould be removed\fP are always listed, however many there are\. `\f[CR]\-\-all\fP` adds every snapshot with its reasons, \fBoldest first\fP so the table reads as a timeline \-\- sparse monthly snapshots at the top, thickening into hourly at the bottom\. It is off by default because a host backing up hourly for a month holds well over a hundred\.
.PP
It needs the repository, a credential and a few seconds, like \fBsnapshots\fP and unlike \fBconfig\fP and \fBplan\fP\.
.PP
If the profile declares no `\f[CR]keep\-*\fP` rule under \fB[forget]\fP, rustic refuses the whole operation \-\- \fI"Invalid keep options specified"\fP \-\- and this command says which file is missing one\. Note that \fBkeep\-delete\fP does not count: it is prune's grace period, not a retention rule, and rustic rejects a `\f[CR][forget]\fP` that declares only that\. There is deliberately no \fB\-\-json\fP; a schema is a promise, and nobody asked for a machine\-readable retention feed\. \fBplan\fP prints the command line if you want to parse rustic's own JSON directly\.
.RE

.SH "DOCTOR"
\fBdoctor\fP
.RS
.PP
Check the things \fBconfig \-\-check\fP structurally cannot\.
.PP
\fBconfig \-\-check\fP is hermetic by design \-\- no repository, no network, no other tool \-\- which is what makes it safe to run anywhere and is also its ceiling\. The failures worth catching here are configurations that are individually correct and wrong only \fIin combination\fP, so the evidence for them lives outside any config file: in the repository, in another tool's schedules, or on disk\. That division is why both commands exist\.
.RS
.PP
.nf
rusticprofile doctor                 # local checks only, instant
rusticprofile doctor \-\-repository    # also ask the repository
rusticprofile doctor \-\-json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Two checks always run, and both are local:
\fBlock\-authority\fP
.RS
.PP
Whether a \fBrestic\fP prune schedule is registered on this host while rustic writes the repository\. `\f[CR]restic prune\fP` deletes packs immediately and rustic takes no lock, which is the one combination measured to leave a repository failing `\f[CR]restic check\fP`\. A predecessor unit that is installed but \fBdisabled\fP reports `\f[CR]ok\fP` and is still listed \-\- it is one `\f[CR]systemctl enable\fP` away from mattering\.
.PP
This is a \fBper\-host\fP check\. It cannot see other machines, and running it on one host says nothing about the rest of a fleet\.
.RE
\fBsecrets\-present\fP
.RS
.PP
Whether the credential files the rustic profile names actually exist and can be opened\. The contents are never read\. A profile using \fBpassword\-command\fP reports `\f[CR]ok\fP` with nothing to check, which is the recommended configuration rather than an unverifiable one\.
.PP
This is pre\-flight ergonomics, not a data\-safety fix: a missing passphrase already fails loudly when a backup runs\. What it does not do is fail while anyone is watching\.
.RE
.PP
One check is opt\-in, because it is the only one that needs the network, a credential and several seconds \-\- and the only one that can fail for reasons unrelated to what it asks:
\fBretention\-authority\fP (\fB\-\-repository\fP)\fP
.RS
.PP
Whether any host has restic\-written snapshots \fInewer\fP than its rustic cutover, which is what a second retention authority looks like from inside the repository\. rusticprofile labels every snapshot set and the predecessor labels none, so the writer is a recorded fact rather than an inference\.
.PP
The test is \fBordering, not presence\fP\. A migrated host legitimately holds years of unlabelled history, so warning on any mix would warn forever; the signal is an unlabelled snapshot dated after the oldest labelled one\. The query is scoped by the profile's own `\f[CR]filter\-hosts\fP`, so on a fleet configuration it sees the local host\.
.RE
.PP
A finding is `\f[CR]ok\fP`, `\f[CR]warn\fP`, or `\f[CR]unknown\fP` \-\- the last meaning the check could not run\. "Could not look" is reported separately from "looked and it was fine", and only `\f[CR]warn\fP` affects the exit status\.
.RE

.SH "SECRETS"
.PP
rusticprofile stores no secrets and has no secret configuration\. The repository password, master key and any cloud credentials belong to \fBrustic\fP(1), which offers `\f[CR]\-\-password\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-password\-file\fP` and `\f[CR]\-\-password\-command\fP` (and `\f[CR]\-\-key\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-key\-file\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-key\-command\fP`), any of which may be set in its own config file\.
.PP
Prefer \fBpassword\-command\fP\. rustic spawns that command itself, so the secret never enters rusticprofile's memory, argument list or environment\. rusticprofile never emits a credential\-bearing flag into a command line \-\- a process list is world\-readable, and rustic's own documentation warns that `\f[CR]\-\-password\fP` can expose the password there\.
.PP
Anything rusticprofile prints is masked as a backstop\. Masking is by variable name and distinguishes a secret from a location: `\f[CR]RUSTIC_PASSWORD\fP` is hidden while `\f[CR]RUSTIC_PASSWORD_FILE\fP` is shown, since a path reveals nothing and is usually the detail worth seeing\. A `\f[CR]_COMMAND\fP` variant is hidden, because such a command can embed the secret inline and nothing can distinguish that from a keyring lookup\. The replacement marker is fixed, never a run of characters matching the secret's length\.

.SH "INTERPOLATION"
.PP
Strings in the configuration may contain `\f[CR]${\.\.\.}\fP` references\. This is a closed set, not a template language: there are no conditionals, functions, pipelines or loops\. An unrecognised name is an error naming the offending key, never an empty string\.
.PP
`\f[CR]${host}\fP`, `\f[CR]${host_short}\fP`, `\f[CR]${job}\fP`, `\f[CR]${profile}\fP`, `\f[CR]${config_dir}\fP`, `\f[CR]${state_dir}\fP`, `\f[CR]${temp_dir}\fP`, `\f[CR]${os}\fP`, `\f[CR]${arch}\fP`, `\f[CR]${env:NAME}\fP` and `\f[CR]${date:FORMAT}\fP` are recognised\. `\f[CR]$${\fP` produces a literal `\f[CR]${\fP`\.
.PP
`\f[CR]${job}\fP` and `\f[CR]${profile}\fP` have no value outside a job and are errors there\. `\f[CR]${env:NAME}\fP` is an error when the variable is unset\. `\f[CR]${date:FORMAT}\fP` is validated when the configuration loads but resolved when the job runs, so that a generated unit file carries the reference rather than one day's date\.

.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
The contract the implemented commands follow:
\fB0\fP
.RS
Success, including a run that completed with warnings\.
.RE
\fB1\fP
.RS
A run failed \-\- or, for the read\-only commands that invoke rustic, the query could not be completed: rustic would not start, exited non\-zero, or returned output that could not be read\. \fBsnapshots\fP passes rustic's own exit code through instead, since inventing a verdict there would be a second opinion nobody asked for\.
.RE
\fB2\fP
.RS
The configuration is invalid\. Reported before any process is spawned, with every violation listed at once, so a config error is never confused with a backup failure\.
.RE
\fB3\fP
.RS
\fBdoctor\fP found something a human should look at\. Distinct from both success and a configuration error, so a monitor can tell "healthy", "needs attention" and "broken configuration" apart\. A check that could not run does not set it\.
.RE
\fB130\fP
.RS
Interrupted\.
.RE

.SH "FILES"
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rusticprofile/jobs\.yaml\fP
.RS
.PP
Job definitions: which jobs exist, their operations, host gating and schedule\.
.RE
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rustic/PROFILE\.toml\fP
.RS
.PP
rustic's own configuration, which owns all backup detail\. rusticprofile reads it read\-only, to verify that every snapshot\-set name it is about to pass actually exists\.
.RE
\fB~/Library/LaunchAgents/local\.rusticprofile\.\fBJOB\fP\.plist\fP
.RS
.PP
The launchd agent on macOS, written by \fBschedule\fP and removed by \fBunschedule\fP\. `\f[CR]permission: system\fP` uses `\f[CR]/Library/LaunchDaemons\fP` instead\. On Linux the equivalents are `\f[CR]~/\.config/systemd/user/rusticprofile\-\fP`\fIJOB\fP`\f[CR]\.{service,timer}\fP`\.
.RE
\fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/rusticprofile/tasks/\fBJOB\fP\.xml\fP
.RS
.PP
On Windows, the task definition \fBschedule\fP generated\. Unlike the Unix backends this file is \fInot\fP the installation \-\- `\f[CR]schtasks\fP` copies the definition into the Task Scheduler service's own store, and the registration at `\f[CR]\rusticprofile\\fP`\fIJOB\fP is what actually runs\. This copy is a record of what was registered, and deleting it does not unschedule anything\.
.RE
\fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/rusticprofile/status/\fBJOB\fP\.json\fP
.RS
.PP
When \fIJOB\fP last ran, and when it last \fBsucceeded\fP\. Written after every run, atomically\. `\f[CR]last_success\fP` is carried forward across a failed run on purpose: the useful question is not "did the last attempt work?" but "when did this last actually work?", and only that field can reveal a job which has quietly stopped working \-\- a failing run is loud, a run that never happens is not\. \fBstatus\fP displays both\.
.RE
\fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/rusticprofile/\fP
.RS
.PP
Where `\f[CR]${state_dir}\fP` points, and where run logs belong\. Logs are \fBstate, not configuration\fP \-\- the XDG Base Directory specification names them as the example of what `\f[CR]XDG_STATE_HOME\fP` is for\. Pointing a `\f[CR]log:\fP` at `\f[CR]${config_dir}\fP` instead is also self\-defeating when `\f[CR]~/\.config\fP` is one of your backup sources: the job then appends to a directory it is in the middle of backing up, and the rustic profile needs an exclusion to compensate\.
.RE
.PP
The variables are honoured where they are set and fall back to `\f[CR]~/\.config\fP` and `\f[CR]~/\.local/state\fP` where they are not \-\- \fBon macOS and Windows as well as Linux\fP, rather than `\f[CR]~/Library/Application Support\fP` or `\f[CR]%APPDATA%\fP`\. A relative value is ignored, as the specification requires; note that on Windows a path without a drive letter counts as relative, so a rooted\-but\-driveless `\f[CR]log:\fP` is refused there\. This is deliberate rather than unidiomatic: `\f[CR]jobs\.yaml\fP` is meant to be byte\-identical across a fleet, and a location that varied by operating system would make one line of one file resolve to two different places\.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBrustic\fP(1), \fBsystemd\.timer\fP(5), \fBlaunchd\.plist\fP(5)

